[% META
   title = "2026-08-18 — Git Worktree port-allocation collision fix"
   description = "Fix: Util::Git next_free_port / create_worktree could hand a new branch an already-taken port (observed 4004 reused). Allocator now reads worktrees.json fresh from disk and merges on write; registry corrected to git=4004, planning=4005."
   roles = "admin,developer,devops"
   TemplateType = "Documentation"
   category = "git,infrastructure,changelog"
   page_version = "1.0"
   last_updated = "Mon Aug 18 08:00:00 UTC 2026"
   project_id   = "238"
   project_code = "GITWT-NEW"
   site_specific = "false"
%]

<div class="container">
    <h1 class="doc-h1">2026-08-18 — Git Worktree port-allocation collision fix</h1>

    <div class="last-updated" doc-muted>
        Last Updated: 2026-08-18 |
        Version: 1.0 |
        Owning plan: <a href="/Documentation/git_worktree_new_system_plan" class="doc-link">git_worktree_new_system_plan (v0.2.1)</a> —
        project <a href="/project/details?project_id=238" class="doc-link">#238 (GITWT-NEW)</a>
    </div>

    <div class="row"><div class="col-100">
        <h2 class="doc-h2">Symptom</h2>
        <p class="doc-body">Creating a new worktree branch assigned it a port that was <strong>already in use</strong> by another worktree. Concretely, a new branch came up on <strong>4004</strong> — the port the <code>git</code> worktree (branch <code>git-dev</code>) was already serving — instead of the next free port. The Branch Servers list then showed two entries pointing at the same port.</p>
    </div></div>

    <div class="row"><div class="col-100">
        <h2 class="doc-h2">Root cause</h2>
        <p class="doc-body"><code>Util::Git-&gt;next_free_port</code> (and <code>create_worktree</code>) read the <strong>module-level cached</strong> <code>worktrees.json</code> map (<code>$_wt_config</code>) rather than the live file. On a multi-worker Catalyst server each worker holds its own cache; once primed it was never invalidated when another worker (or the dashboard) wrote a new branch+port to the JSON. The allocator therefore kept serving a stale port set and re-issued an occupied port. The underlying "skip any port already in the JSON" scan was correct in isolation — the cache made it blind to reality.</p>
    </div></div>

    <div class="row"><div class="col-100">
        <h2 class="doc-h2">Fix</h2>
        <ul class="doc-body">
            <li><code>_worktree_config</code> is now <strong>mtime-aware</strong>: re-reads the file whenever the on-disk mtime changes.</li>
            <li>Added <code>_worktree_json_fresh</code> — a cache-<em>bypassing</em> direct read of the JSON — and routed the <strong>allocation</strong> path (<code>next_free_port</code> + <code>create_worktree</code>) through it. The allocator never trusts the cache, even for two creates inside the same 1-second mtime window.</li>
            <li><code>create_worktree</code> re-reads the live JSON right before writing, discards a port claimed in the interim, and <strong>merges</strong> its branch into the current on-disk map (instead of overwriting the file), so a concurrent writer's entry is not lost.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="doc-body">Files: <code>lib/Comserv/Util/Git.pm</code> (no schema / no API signature change).</p>
    </div></div>

    <div class="row"><div class="col-100">
        <h2 class="doc-h2">Tests</h2>
        <ul class="doc-body">
            <li>New <code>t/util_git_next_free_port.t</code> — 7 cases: collision (4001–4004 taken → 4005), stale-cache / out-of-band claim (4002 taken → 4003), existing owner keeps its port. All pass.</li>
            <li><code>t/util_git_create_worktree.t</code> — 13 cases, unchanged behaviour, still green.</li>
        </ul>
    </div></div>

    <div class="row"><div class="col-100">
        <h2 class="doc-h2">Registry correction (same day)</h2>
        <p class="doc-body"><code>worktrees.json</code> was out of sync with the running servers: the <code>git</code> worktree (branch <code>git-dev</code>) was bound to <strong>4004</strong> but had no JSON entry, while <code>planning</code> wrongly claimed 4004. Corrected the registry to match the live listeners:</p>
        <table class="doc-table">
            <tr><th class="doc-th">Branch</th><th class="doc-th">Port (before)</th><th class="doc-th">Port (after)</th></tr>
            <tr><td class="doc-td">git (git-dev)</td><td class="doc-td">— (no entry)</td><td class="doc-td">4004</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="doc-td">planning</td><td class="doc-td">4004</td><td class="doc-td">4005</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="doc-td">InventoryAccounting / DockerHA / 3d</td><td class="doc-td">4001 / 4002 / 4003</td><td class="doc-td">unchanged</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p class="doc-body">Final map: 4001 InventoryAccounting, 4002 DockerHA, 4003 3d, 4004 git, 4005 planning. No two entries share a port.</p>
    </div></div>

    <div class="row"><div class="col-100">
        <h2 class="doc-h2">Back to plan</h2>
        <p class="doc-body">See <a href="/Documentation/git_worktree_new_system_plan#changelog" class="doc-link">git_worktree_new_system_plan §12 Changelog (v0.2.1)</a>.</p>
    </div></div>
</div>
